# Material Design

Use material-inspired structure, elevation, state layers, and motion without claiming complete conformance to Google's Material specification or importing a component suite by default.

- **Palette:** Light background `#FFFBFE`, foreground `#1D1B20`, primary `#6750A4`, secondary `#625B71`, surface `#FFFBFE`, border `#79747E`, focus `#381E72`. Dark background `#1C1B1F`, foreground `#E6E1E5`, surface `#211F26`, primary `#D0BCFF`, secondary `#CCC2DC`.
- **Type:** Roboto when approved or system sans; strong title/body/label roles with consistent line heights and weights.
- **Surfaces:** Tonal containers, 12–16px shape scale, 1px outlines, and a small elevation ladder. Avoid arbitrary one-off shadows.
- **Components:** Filled/tonal/outlined buttons, floating or outlined fields, accessible navigation rails, cards, dialogs, tabs, snackbars, and data tables. Use shared state-layer tokens.
- **Interaction:** Hover, focus, press, drag, and selected states use predictable overlays and elevation changes. Disabled states use dedicated content/container tokens and native semantics. Motion explains continuity, not decoration; focus remains explicit.
- **Responsive:** Shift bottom navigation to rail/sidebar as content warrants; preserve consistent grid and touch sizes.
- **Accessibility:** Labels must not rely on placeholders; errors remain associated with fields. Icons require names and minimum targets. Verify tonal combinations rather than assuming palette compatibility.
- **Theme/performance:** Both themes have separate tonal surface roles. Implement ordinary states with CSS; do not add a full third-party library unless the project needs it. Respect reduced motion.

Representative preview: application shell with tonal navigation, form field, elevated card, tabs, snackbar, and structured data list.
